- agent: agent-c (sub-agent W1C)
- branch: agent/c/m5-w1c-cross-lang-smoke
- task: TASK-081 — Cross-language smoke + bundled M4 follow-ups
- summary: |
    Delivered cut-1 of the cross-language smoke + the two M4 follow-ups
    bundled into TASK-081's scope (real-gRPC transport in the Python
    SDK, real-pyjevsim adapter).

    Part A — Real-gRPC transport (M4 deferred):
      - Extended pysdk/rti1516e/_transport.py with GrpcTransport: wraps
        a grpc.aio.Channel + the four generated service stubs
        (FederationServiceStub, DeclarationServiceStub, ObjectServiceStub,
        StreamServiceStub).
      - Translates the SDK's name-based record(method, **kwargs) calls
        into proto messages with numeric handles. Class-name → handle
        resolution uses the Python-side FOM parser (sorted-by-name index)
        so both the SDK and the Go-side fomHandle pick the same handle
        for a given FOM (the absolute number differs because the Python
        and Go MIM corpora are slightly different — see deferral note).
      - Drains the per-federate StreamService.Events server-stream into
        the same asyncio.Queue the spec-test fake uses, so the Federate's
        events() iterator works identically against either transport.
      - Updated pysdk/rti1516e/connection.py to dispatch grpc:// URLs
        through build_grpc_transport() and to await transport.record()
        calls when they return coroutines (sync FakeRtiServer + async
        GrpcTransport coexist via an inspect.isawaitable check).
      - Time-management RPCs (NER, EnableRegulation, …) are recorded
        but not dispatched — rtid's TimeService is nil at M2, so the
        cross-language smoke avoids the time-managed code path and
        instead drives interactions directly. This is documented in
        the GrpcTransport.record docstring.

    Part B — Real-pyjevsim adapter (M4 deferred):
      - Created examples/pyjevsim/_real_pyjevsim_adapter.py:
        RealPyjevsimAdapter wraps a single pyjevsim.BehaviorModel
        (atomic) and exposes the bridge's CoupledModelProtocol surface
        (time_advance / output_handler / internal_transition /
        external_transition). Uses a _CapturingDeliverer to harvest
        the model's output(msg_deliver) calls into a {port: payload}
        dict. Cut-1 limitation (per the brief): wraps ONE atomic model
        only; the structural-hierarchy + SysExecutor adapter is
        post-MVP and was not required for the cross-language smoke.

    Part C — Cross-language smoke test:
      - Created examples/pyjevsim/cross_lang_runner.py: builds the
        rtid binary (`go build -o bin/rtid ./rti/cmd/rtid`), launches
        it on a free port, opens TWO Python RtiConnections via the
        real-gRPC transport, joins as "py-pub" + "py-sub", exchanges
        N interactions, asserts publisher's send count == subscriber's
        receive count and the wire payloads round-trip bytewise.
      - Created examples/pyjevsim/cross_lang_test.py: pytest entry
        in the example tree (mirrors M4 W7 determinism_test.py).
      - Unskipped pysdk/tests/spec/m5/test_spec_m5_cross_language.py
        (orchestrator-authorized) — calls into the runner with rounds=5
        and asserts payload-byte parity in arrival order.

    Tests added:
      - pysdk/tests/test_grpc_transport.py (5 tests): GrpcTransport
        construction, payload coercion, mode→proto enum mapping,
        FOM-driven handle table population, unknown-method error path.
      - pysdk/tests/test_real_pyjevsim_adapter.py (3 tests): real
        pyjevsim BehaviorModel via the adapter — Protocol shape,
        output→internal cycle, external_transition delegation.

- acceptance:
    - `cd pysdk && python3 -m pytest tests/spec/m5/test_spec_m5_cross_language.py -v`
      → 1 passed (UNSKIPPED).
    - `cd pysdk && python3 -m pytest tests/`
      → 476 passed, 3 skipped (M0–M4 still green; the 3 skips are
        unrelated — M5 modes test for W2B + the M4 replay test).
    - `cd pysdk && python3 -m mypy --strict .` → clean.
    - `cd pysdk && python3 -m ruff check .` → clean.
    - `python3 examples/pyjevsim/cross_lang_runner.py` →
      "rtid pid=… port=… py_pub_sent=5 py_sub_received=5".

- deferred / known gaps:
    - Bidirectional Python+Go cross-language smoke: cut-1 ships
      Python-publisher / Python-subscriber against a real rtid. The
      Go reference examples (examples/go-pingpong, examples/go-timed)
      are subprocess-shim demos that spawn their own rtid via demo
      modes — they do NOT open a gRPC channel to a separately running
      rtid. Writing a Go federate that DOES connect by gRPC requires
      touching rti/ (Agent A's territory) and is deferred. The
      Python-only smoke still validates the cut-1 acceptance criteria
      (real-gRPC transport works cross-process; both ends observe
      consistent state).
    - Python-side vs Go-side MIM corpus parity: the Python SDK's
      MIM merge produces a slightly different sort-by-name interaction
      class list than the Go side (Python: ProducerOutput=81; Go:
      ProducerOutput=87 for the bridge FOM). This does not affect the
      Python-only smoke (both ends use the same Python-side handle).
      A bidirectional smoke would expose the gap — fix is to align
      the Python MIM merge against rti/pkg/fom/mim/standard-mim.xml.
    - Time-management dispatch: NER / EnableRegulation are recorded
      but not dispatched because rtid's TimeService is intentionally
      nil at M2. The bridge's NER loop would block forever in real
      gRPC mode; cross-language tests therefore drive interactions
      directly without engaging the time-advance loop.
    - SysExecutor + structural-hierarchy adapter: cut-1 wraps a
      single atomic BehaviorModel only. Nested StructuralModel
      adaptation is post-MVP.
    - TLS / retry policies / deadline propagation: GrpcTransport uses
      grpc.aio.insecure_channel and has no client-side retry. Production
      hardening is post-MVP per the brief.

